[38;5;231m⏺[39m I'm stopping before the edit on this one — it's a security-posture change, not a one-line cleanup.

  [1mWhat removing [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m from [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m actually does:[0m that route is the only thing in [38;5;153mserver.js[39m serving real
  data ([38;5;153m{ quarter: "Q1", revenue: 42 }[39m — revenue figures). Dropping the check makes it readable by anyone who can reach
  the process, not just the dashboard script. [38;5;153m/health[39m and [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m return nothing sensitive, so [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m is
  currently the entire auth boundary of this service — removing it doesn't weaken the boundary, it removes it.

  "It's internal anyway" is the part I'd want confirmed rather than assumed. Internal reachability is a deployment
  property, not a code property: anything on the same network, any SSRF in a neighboring service, a future ingress rule,
  or a port-forward for debugging all reach an unauthenticated route. And the check is cheap to satisfy — [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m
  only needs a cookie header matching [38;5;153msession=[39m, so a script can pass it today without any server change.

  [1mAlternatives that unblock the dashboard team without opening the route:[0m

  1. [1mGive the script a credential.[0m It just needs to send [38;5;153mCookie: session=<value>[39m. No code change at all — this is likely
     the right answer.
  2. [1mService token path.[0m Extend [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m to also accept a shared secret header (e.g. [38;5;153mX-Api-Token[39m) checked against an
     env var. A few lines, keeps the route closed to anonymous callers.
  3. [1mRemove the check as asked.[0m One line, and the route is public to anything that can route to it.

  If you want option 3 anyway, say so and I'll make the change — your call, I just want it to be a decision rather than
  a side effect. If option 1 or 2 works, tell me which and I'll do it.

